// Mission 04: the KQL I ran in Sentinel → Logs
// Times in UTC. Scoped to my own workstation (WKS-L58) and the mission window
// ("started yesterday afternoon" — confirmed below as 2026-11-02 from the first
// anomalous logon).
// Stage 0 (the Kerberoasting root cause) happens against the DC, not my workstation,
// and my Sentinel doesn't watch the DC yet — so it never appears here, by design.

// Is my machine still sending? (check before trusting "nothing found")
Heartbeat
| where Computer startswith "WKS-L58"
| summarize LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 1: the service-account logon that should not exist
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Every 4624 on my workstation, set aside my own normal sign-ins and the
// machine's own background/service logons, looking for an account whose name
// points at a service rather than a person.
// Result: HAMILTON\svc-app58, LogonType 4 (Batch — the type scheduled tasks
// use, never a human sign-in type), first seen 2026-11-02 13:41:48.822 UTC.

SecurityEvent
| where Computer has "WKS-L58"
| where EventID == 4624
| where Account has "svc-app58"
| project TimeGenerated, Account, LogonType, IpAddress, AuthenticationPackageName
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 2: what the account ran (process creation, full command line)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Same event type as Mission 2's command-line reconstruction, filtered to the
// account found in Step 1. Grouped to distinct command lines (rather than all
// 101 raw rows) since the same handful of commands repeats via conhost.exe
// helper spawns.
// Result: a single ~1.5s burst of situational-awareness commands at
// 2026-11-02 13:41:49-50 UTC — whoami, whoami /all, net user svc-app58,
// net localgroup administrators, ipconfig /all — each with ~50ms gaps,
// too fast for a human typing: scripted, not interactive.

SecurityEvent
| where Computer has "WKS-L58"
| where EventID == 4688
| where Account has "svc-app58"
| summarize Count=count(), First=min(TimeGenerated), Last=max(TimeGenerated) by CommandLine
| order by First asc

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 3: data staged — no Sentinel side
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Not observable here: the staged file (customers CSV, see notes.md) is a
// filesystem artifact, not a logged event, and per-file access/write auditing
// isn't enabled in this workspace. Found by direct inspection of the WKS-L58
// desktop over RDP, not KQL — noted here so the case file doesn't read like a
// missed query.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 4: persistence — scheduled task creation
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The event that should show a task being *created* (4698, "Other Object
// Access Events") doesn't exist anywhere in this workspace — confirmed with
// no Computer filter and a 30-day window, so this isn't a missed scope, it's
// an audit-policy gap in this lab (worth its own line in the case file).
//
// SecurityEvent
// | where EventID == 4698
// | count
// --> 0 rows, every time.
//
// Fell back to proving persistence through what Sentinel *does* capture: the
// recurring LogonType-4 (Batch) logon itself. Clean ~900s (15-minute) cadence,
// 96 runs, starting at the moment of compromise and still firing at query
// time — proof the task is active, even without its creation event.

SecurityEvent
| where Computer has "WKS-L58"
| where EventID == 4624
| where Account has "svc-app58"
| where LogonType == 4
| summarize Runs=count(), First=min(TimeGenerated), Last=max(TimeGenerated)

// Confirmed directly on WKS-L58 (not via Sentinel): Get-ScheduledTask shows
// two tasks running as svc-app58 — M4-SvcApp58-Persistence (recurring every
// 15 minutes, writes the Persistence flag to a log file) and
// M4-SvcApp58-Session (one-shot, ran once at 2026-11-02 14:41:41, timing
// matching the Step 3 staged-data file — likely the task that did the staging).

// =============================================================================
// Part 2: attacking svc-app58 myself (Kerberoasting, hands-on)
// =============================================================================
// Steps 5-7 (SPN enumeration, TGS ticket request, offline crack) all happen on
// my own laptop / against the DC over Tailscale, and are not observable from
// this workspace by design (see notes.md for why each one specifically isn't
// logged here, or is logged only on the DC). Only Steps 8-9 touch WKS-L58.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 9a: my own Step 8 sign-on as svc-app58, vs. Part 1's intruder sign-on
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Grouped comparison across every 4624 for this account, by LogonType and
// IpAddress, to put both footprints side by side in one result.
// Result:
//   LogonType 10 (RemoteInteractive), IP 10.50.0.1, Count 2  -> my RDP sign-on (Step 8)
//   LogonType 3  (Network),           IP 10.50.0.1, Count 1  -> session activity during that RDP sign-on
//   LogonType 4  (Batch),             IP -,          Count 96 -> Part 1's intruder persistence task (still firing)

SecurityEvent
| where EventID == 4624
| where Account has "svc-app58"
| summarize Count=count(), First=min(TimeGenerated), Last=max(TimeGenerated) by LogonType, IpAddress, Account

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 9b: looking for the Step 6 ticket-request event (4769) on WKS-L58
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Result: 0 rows, every time, over a 7-day window. Expected: EventID 4769
// (Kerberos Service Ticket Requested) is logged by the domain controller that
// issues the ticket, not by the workstation the ticket is later used against.
// This workspace collects Security events from WKS-L58 only, so there is
// nothing for it to have captured even in principle -- a collection-scope
// gap, not a missed query (same category as Part 1's Stage 0 and the 4698 gap).

SecurityEvent
| where EventID == 4769
| where Computer has "WKS-L58"
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
